# {{NAME}}

A decompilation project for the Nintendo DS ROM `{{IMAGE}}`
(sha1 `{{SHA1}}`), bootstrapped by `romwright new-project` (romwright {{ROMWRIGHT}}).

The retail ROM is not part of this repository and neither is anything derived from
its bytes (`extract/`, `build/` are ignored): put your own dump at `orig/{{IMAGE}}`
and the project rebuilds it.

## Quick start

```sh
python3 -m pip install -r requirements.txt   # ninja
python3 configure.py                         # fetch tools, extract the ROM, dsd init, merge romwright's symbols
ninja                                        # delink, link (mwldarm), rebuild the ROM, compare with the retail sha1
```

A fresh project rebuilds the whole ROM from delinked objects and the last step
prints `OK build/{{IMAGE}} sha1 {{SHA1}}`. If it prints `MISMATCH`, the message gives
the number of differing bytes and the ROM region of the first one.

An *encrypted* dump (a retail cartridge dump whose secure area is still encrypted) also
needs the ARM7 BIOS, which is never stored here: `python3 configure.py --arm7-bios arm7.bin`
(or `ROMWRIGHT_ARM7_BIOS`).

## How the ROM is built

[`dsd`](https://github.com/AetiasHax/ds-decomp) (ds-decomp, MIT) with its embedded
[ds-rom](https://github.com/AetiasHax/ds-rom) does the DS-specific work:

1. `dsd rom extract` splits the ROM into the ARM9 program (decompressed), its overlays,
   the ARM7 program, banner, header and files (`extract/`);
2. `dsd init` analyses ARM9, ITCM/DTCM and every overlay into `config/dsd/arm9/`
   (`config.yaml`, and per module `delinks.txt` = its sections, `relocs.txt`, `symbols.txt`);
3. `tools/merge_symbols.py` merges romwright's inventory (`config/romwright/<module>.symbols.txt`,
   {{ARM9_FUNCTIONS}} ARM9 and {{OVERLAY_FUNCTIONS}} overlay functions in {{MODULES}} modules) into dsd's `symbols.txt`:
   names romwright found replace dsd's `func_...` defaults, functions dsd missed are added
   (`configure.py --no-add-functions` renames only), and relocations dsd could not attribute to one overlay are dropped (the linker
   would otherwise pick an overlay at random: a table of overlay function pointers must keep the retail bytes).
   `build/merge_report.json` has the counts. The ARM7 program is not part of dsd's model; its
   functions are in `config/romwright/arm7.symbols.txt` for reference;
4. `ninja`: `dsd delink`, `dsd lcf`, `mwldarm` (through wibo), `dsd rom config`, `dsd rom build`,
   then `tools/romfix.py` writes the two header fields ds-rom cannot recompute from a decrypted
   dump: the retail secure-area CRC (`config/header.json`) and the header CRC.

